; ============================================================================
; A NULL IS ASSERTABLE -- AIF-091, the coverage the null lane never had
; ============================================================================
;
; WHY THIS SPEC EXISTS.
;
; Between 2026-09-04 and 2026-09-05 this engine learned to CREATE a nullable
; VFP table, WRITE a null, READ a null, SEE a null (LIST prints .NULL.) and
; FILTER on a null (LIST FOR ISNULL(f)). Every one of those legs was proven
; against bytes Visual FoxPro itself wrote, and NONE of them is reachable by
; REGRESSION ALL, because no .dts spec asserts a null. The capability shipped
; five commits ahead of its coverage.
;
; That is the whole subject of this file: the suite could not tell a build that
; writes nulls from a build that silently drops them on the floor.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; HOW A NULL IS ASSERTED HERE, AND WHY *NOT* WITH `? ISNULL(f)`
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; The obvious spec is `? "NL_T1:" + ISNULL(VNAME)`, and it was the plan. IT IS
; NOT USED, and the reason is measured from the code rather than assumed:
;
;   `?` is a SHORTCUT for FORMULA (shortcut_resolver.hpp: { "?", "FORMULA" }).
;   FORMULA calls xexpr::evaluate_expression -> dottalk::expr::eval_rhs.
;   eval_rhs tries its OWN scalar parser first (rhs_eval.cpp), and that parser
;   is where string concatenation with `+` lives. Its function table is
;   call_string_builtin / call_date_builtin / call_numeric_builtin /
;   call_custom_builtin. ISNULL IS IN NONE OF THEM -- it exists only in
;   FunctionCall::evalString (cli/expr/eval.cpp), which is the PREDICATE AST.
;   In that AST `+` is Arith, and Arith::evalString returns
;   std::to_string(evalNumber(...)) -- a NUMBER, not a concatenation.
;
; So the marker path and the ISNULL path do not meet. This is the same shape
; the catalogue already records one entry above ISNULL's own:
;
;   "RECNO() renders EMPTY in a '?' marker inside a .dts script -- measured
;    over four specs -- and STR() does not rescue it. Spec markers compare
;    FIELD VALUES for this reason."
;
; That note was written about RECNO. It is true of every FunctionCategory::Cursor
; function for the same structural reason, ISNULL included. A spec built on
; `? "NAME:" + ISNULL(f)` would therefore have rendered a label with nothing
; after it, or a number -- and an unreadable marker is not a red one.
;
; WHAT THIS FILE DOES INSTEAD. It asserts nulls through the path that IS wired:
; the FOR predicate. LOCATE FOR ISNULL(<field>) goes through compile_predicate
; -> compile_where_program -> the AST where ISNULL lives, which is exactly the
; path LIST FOR / COUNT FOR use and the path the feature was built for. The
; cursor lands on a row, and the arm then reads an ORDINARY CHARACTER FIELD of
; that row. Every marker below is a FIELD-VALUE comparison, per the
; FIELDMGR_APPEND doctrine, and not one of them depends on the marker path
; being able to see a null.
;
; Two ungraded PROBES at the very end of the file record what the marker path
; actually does with ISNULL, bare and concatenated, so the paragraph above is
; measured on every run instead of inherited from this comment. They are placed
; last so that nothing depends on them.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE DISCRIMINATOR, AND WHY THE FIXTURE IS ORDERED THE WAY IT IS
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; A NULL CELL AND A BLANK CELL BOTH EVALUATE TO THE EMPTY STRING. That is not a
; defect, it is the reason ISNULL has to exist: no value comparison can tell
; them apart, which is why ISNULL refuses to evaluate its argument at all.
;
; So a spec that only nulls a cell and finds it proves nothing -- it passes on
; a build where ISNULL answers .T. for anything empty. The fixture is built to
; make that build fail:
;
;   rec 1  VNAME "alpha"    PLAIN "control"   -- populated, never touched
;   rec 2  VNAME <blank>    PLAIN "blankvn"   -- BLANK AND NOT NULL
;   rec 3  VNAME "gamma"    PLAIN "nullvn"    -- VNAME nulled, bare NULL
;   rec 4  VNAME "delta"    PLAIN "nullid"    -- ID nulled
;   rec 5  VNAME "epsilon"  PLAIN "nulltwo"   -- VNAME nulled, .NULL. spelling
;
; REC 2 PRECEDES REC 3 ON PURPOSE. LOCATE returns the FIRST match from the top,
; so a build that confuses blank with null lands on rec 2 and NL_T1 reads
; "blankvn" instead of "nullvn". The arm is positive -- it names the row it
; expects -- because the negative form ("not blankvn") also passes when LOCATE
; went nowhere, hit end of file, or failed outright.
;
; NL_T5 and NL_T6 are the pair that states the problem out loud: the nulled cell
; and the blank cell BOTH read empty by value. They are asserted, not implied,
; so a reader can see that NL_T1 separates two rows that nothing else here can.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT ELSE IS ASSERTED AND WHY EACH ARM CAN GO RED
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; NL_T2 -- BIT INDEX. ID is field 1 and VNAME is field 2, and in a VFP null
;   bitmap they do not own adjacent bits: VNAME is a Varchar, so it owns a
;   VARLENGTH bit as well as a NULL bit and the assignment runs
;   bit0 = ID null, bit1 = VNAME varlength, bit2 = VNAME null. Two of this
;   lane's own commits exist because that order was got wrong (45af198b5, the
;   order was backwards; dfd7cf15e, the flags byte was read from offset 23
;   where the format puts it at 18). Null the NUMERIC and this arm demands the
;   cursor land on rec 4 -- an off-by-one in the bit assignment lands it on
;   rec 3 or nowhere.
;
; NL_T3, NL_T4, NL_T7 -- ISOLATION. Nulling a cell must change exactly one bit.
;   T3 and T7 read the OTHER FIELD of the same row; T4 reads the SAME FIELD of
;   another row. The bitmap is fully recomputed on every write (record_view.cpp),
;   which is the correct design and also the one that can erase a neighbour --
;   6a10c9353 exists because a write was erasing the row's null bitmap.
;
; NL_T8, NL_T9, NL_T12 -- THE WRITE REACHED THE DISK. Everything above them is
;   satisfied by an in-memory bit that a close would discard. These three close
;   the table and reopen it, which is the only question a regression suite can
;   ask that a staged row cannot answer for itself.
;
; NL_T10, NL_T11 -- THE BIT CLEARS. A null that cannot be replaced by a value is
;   half a feature. T10 reads the new value back; T11 is the stronger half --
;   with rec 3 cleared, LOCATE FOR ISNULL(VNAME) must now find REC 5, and rec 5
;   was nulled with the DOTTED spelling `.NULL.`. So T11 proves three things at
;   once: rec 3's bit was actually cleared and not merely overwritten in the
;   value area, rec 5's bit was set by the second spelling, and the two rows'
;   bits are independent. If `.NULL.` were silently ignored, T11 reds.
;
; NL_T13, NL_T14 -- REFUSAL ON A NON-NULLABLE FIELD. PLAIN is C(8) with no null
;   flag, so there is no bit to record a null in and REPLACE refuses. Neither
;   arm claims the refusal message appeared -- console text is not readable by a
;   marker. They ask the answerable questions instead: did the value survive
;   (T13), and did the refusal leave the rest of the bitmap alone (T14). A
;   refusal that corrupted the bitmap on its way out would pass T13 and red T14.
;
; NL_T15, NL_T16 -- REFUSAL UNDER TABLE BUFFERING. The table buffer stores one
;   VALUE STRING per field and has no way to say NULL; buffering "" would commit
;   a blank that reads back not-null, so REPLACE ... WITH NULL is refused while
;   buffering is on. Same two answerable questions. These arms run LAST because
;   they touch a session-scoped setting, and the file turns buffering back OFF
;   before it closes -- a spec that leaves a setting flipped changes the next
;   spec's answers, which is how a suite starts depending on its running order.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT THIS SPEC DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CLAIM
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; * THAT NO OTHER ROW IS NULL. No marker in this language can assert an absence
;   (USE_AGAIN, three cuts) and an ERRORED MARKER PRINTS NOTHING RATHER THAN
;   GOING RED, so absence is proven only by contrast. Every arm here names a row
;   it expects to find.
; * THAT LIST PRINTS .NULL. That is real and was proven by hand (11b40895a),
;   and it is CONSOLE TEXT. No marker can read it. Stated rather than implied.
; * ANYTHING ABOUT NULL ORDERING UNDER AN INDEX. This table is opened NOINDEX
;   throughout. What a CDX/CNX/LMDB backend makes of a null key is unclaimed by
;   this lane and must not be inferred from a green run here.
; * ANYTHING ABOUT ISNULL OVER A JOINED OR TUPLE ROW SOURCE. One row source is
;   exercised: an open area.
; * ANYTHING ABOUT WHAT VFP'S OWN APPEND BLANK WRITES INTO THE BITMAP. We write
;   zero bits; that is this house's choice and it is not yet measured against
;   VFP. Rec 2 is blank because nothing wrote it, and no arm here reads its
;   null bit.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; MARKER COUNT: 21 GRADED LINES -- 5 guards (NL_G*) and 16 arms (NL_T*).
; COUNT THEM. An errored marker prints nothing rather than going red, so a
; transcript with 20 is a spec that lost a claim, not a spec that passed.
; Four further lines (NL_P*) are UNGRADED PROBES and are not part of the 21.
;
; If any NL_G* reds, treat every NL_T* as UNPROVEN: the arms read fields of
; rows the guards are what establish.
;
; Disposable table, rebuilt every run. Leaves NULLSPEC.dbf in the sandbox, the
; same way set_deleted_visibility.dts leaves SDVIS.dbf -- CREATE overwrites it
; on the next run.
; ============================================================================

; ============================================================================
; FIRST RUN -- 2026-09-05, build Sep 05 2026 08:29:36 (11b40895 dirty)
;
;   17 of 21 GREEN. FOUR RED: NL_T11, NL_T12, NL_T14, NL_T16.
;
; THE FOUR REDS ARE ONE DEFECT, NOT FOUR. All four assert the same thing --
; that after rec 3's null is replaced by a value, LOCATE FOR ISNULL(VNAME) finds
; REC 5. All four found REC 3, still null.
;
;   A VALUE WRITE DOES NOT CLEAR THE NULL BIT.
;
; DbArea::set() (record_view.cpp:298) writes `_fd[idx]` and never touches
; `_fd_null[idx]`. replaceFieldStored() stages through set(). storeFieldsToBuffer()
; then RECOMPUTES the null bitmap FROM `_fd_null` -- so the write stores the new
; value and re-commits the stale null bit in the same record write. It survives a
; close and reopen, because it reached the disk.
;
; And nothing can undo it: DbArea::replaceFieldNull(field, false) is correct and
; callable, and its ONLY caller in the tree is a unit test
; (test_vfp_set_null.cpp:352, arm C). No command path clears a null. So ONCE A
; CELL IS NULL, THE SHELL CANNOT UN-NULL IT.
;
; CONFIRMED AT THE PROMPT, same build, on the table this script left behind:
;
;     LIST     -> rec 3 VNAME reads  .NULL.
;     GO 3
;     ? VNAME  -> restored
;     ? ISNULL(VNAME) -> .T.
;
; One cell, three instruments, two answers. The bytes hold "restored" and the bit
; says null, and the display believes the bit.
;
; THESE FOUR ARMS ARE NOT RETUNED, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. They assert the
; correct behaviour -- VFP clears NULL when you assign a value -- and they are red
; because the engine is wrong, not because the arms are. Repointing them at the
; measured behaviour would convert a finding into a fixture, which is the defect
; this house keeps finding one layer up. They go green when the engine is fixed;
; until then this file reads 17/21 BY DESIGN and a reader is told why here.
;
; NL_T13 and NL_T15 -- the two refusal arms -- went GREEN, and both refusals
; printed the message they were designed to print. NL_T14 and NL_T16 ride behind
; them and red for the reason above, not because a refusal misbehaved.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; SECOND RUN -- 2026-09-05, build Sep 05 2026 09:20:12. 21 of 21 GREEN.
;
; The engine was fixed the same day: replaceFieldStored()'s stage lambda now
; clears the field's null before writing the value, guarded on fieldIsNullable()
; because setFieldNull() refuses a field with no null flag and an unguarded
; `setFieldNull(f,false) && set(f,v)` would have short-circuited every REPLACE on
; every non-VFP table in the product.
;
; ARM E of dottalkpp_vfp_set_null_test is the byte-level half and landed with it:
; write "ABC" over row 5's null VNAME in nullfix.DBF and the row must become,
; byte for byte, what VFP wrote on row 1 -- bitmap 0x02 and
; 41 42 43 20 20 20 20 20 20 03. Both expectations are read out of the fixture at
; run time. ctest -R vfp: 11/11.
;
; SO THIS FILE'S FIRST ACT WAS TO FIND A DEFECT, AND THAT IS THE ARGUMENT FOR IT.
; The four arms that caught it were written before anyone suspected anything --
; they exist because a null that cannot be cleared is half a feature, not because
; a bug was being chased. Both paragraphs above are kept: a spec that quietly
; becomes all-green loses the only evidence that it was ever worth writing.
;
; STILL NOT ASKED, AND IT IS THE ONE LEG WITH NO INDEPENDENT WITNESS: Visual
; FoxPro has never been asked whether a cell WE UN-NULLED reads as not-null.
; Arm E grades our bytes against bytes VFP wrote, which is strong, but our engine
; still did the writing. Every acceptance .prg in tools/vfp asks the other
; direction -- are the cells we nulled null. A short .prg that opens a cleared
; table and asserts ISNULL() is .F. would close it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; ============================================================================

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX

; ============================================================================
; FIXTURE -- five rows, nothing null yet
; ============================================================================

SELECT 1
CREATE VFP NULLSPEC (ID N(6,0) NULL, VNAME V(10) NULL, PLAIN C(8))

APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 1
REPLACE VNAME WITH "alpha"
REPLACE PLAIN WITH "control"

; rec 2 -- VNAME is NEVER WRITTEN. This is the blank-and-not-null control, and
; it sits ahead of every nulled row so that LOCATE reaches it first.
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 2
REPLACE PLAIN WITH "blankvn"

APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 3
REPLACE VNAME WITH "gamma"
REPLACE PLAIN WITH "nullvn"

APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 4
REPLACE VNAME WITH "delta"
REPLACE PLAIN WITH "nullid"

APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 5
REPLACE VNAME WITH "epsilon"
REPLACE PLAIN WITH "nulltwo"

CLOSE

; ============================================================================
; GUARDS -- the fixture is what the arms below assume it is
; ============================================================================

USE NULLSPEC NOINDEX

GO 1
? "NL_G0_row1_is_the_untouched_control:" + (ALLTRIM(PLAIN) = "control")

GO 2
? "NL_G1_row2_is_blank_before_any_null_exists:" + (ALLTRIM(VNAME) = "")

GO 3
? "NL_G2_row3_holds_a_value_before_it_is_nulled:" + (ALLTRIM(VNAME) = "gamma")

GO 4
? "NL_G3_row4_holds_a_value_before_it_is_nulled:" + (ALLTRIM(VNAME) = "delta")

GO 5
? "NL_G4_row5_holds_a_value_before_it_is_nulled:" + (ALLTRIM(VNAME) = "epsilon")

; ============================================================================
; WRITE THE NULLS -- both spellings, two fields, three rows
; ============================================================================

GO 3
REPLACE VNAME WITH NULL

GO 5
REPLACE VNAME WITH .NULL.

GO 4
REPLACE ID WITH NULL

; ============================================================================
; THE DISCRIMINATOR AND THE ISOLATION ARMS
; ============================================================================

; Rec 2 is blank and comes first. A build that answers ISNULL from emptiness
; lands here and reads "blankvn".
LOCATE FOR ISNULL(VNAME)
? "NL_T1_isnull_finds_the_nulled_row_and_not_the_blank_one:" + (ALLTRIM(PLAIN) = "nullvn")

LOCATE FOR ISNULL(ID)
? "NL_T2_isnull_on_the_numeric_finds_the_row_whose_number_was_nulled:" + (ALLTRIM(PLAIN) = "nullid")

GO 4
? "NL_T3_nulling_id_left_vname_readable_in_the_same_row:" + (ALLTRIM(VNAME) = "delta")

GO 1
? "NL_T4_nulling_vname_left_the_neighbour_row_readable:" + (ALLTRIM(VNAME) = "alpha")

GO 3
? "NL_T5_the_nulled_cell_reads_empty_by_value:" + (ALLTRIM(VNAME) = "")

GO 2
? "NL_T6_the_blank_cell_reads_empty_by_value_too:" + (ALLTRIM(VNAME) = "")

GO 3
? "NL_T7_nulling_vname_left_id_readable_in_the_same_row:" + (ID = 3)

; ============================================================================
; THE NULL REACHED THE DISK
; ============================================================================

CLOSE
USE NULLSPEC NOINDEX

LOCATE FOR ISNULL(VNAME)
? "NL_T8_the_varchar_null_survived_close_and_reopen:" + (ALLTRIM(PLAIN) = "nullvn")

LOCATE FOR ISNULL(ID)
? "NL_T9_the_numeric_null_survived_close_and_reopen:" + (ALLTRIM(PLAIN) = "nullid")

; ============================================================================
; THE BIT CLEARS -- and rec 5, nulled with the DOTTED spelling, is what proves it
; ============================================================================

GO 3
REPLACE VNAME WITH "restored"

GO 3
? "NL_T10_a_written_value_replaced_the_null:" + (ALLTRIM(VNAME) = "restored")

LOCATE FOR ISNULL(VNAME)
? "NL_T11_with_row3_cleared_isnull_finds_row5_nulled_by_the_dotted_spelling:" + (ALLTRIM(PLAIN) = "nulltwo")

CLOSE
USE NULLSPEC NOINDEX

LOCATE FOR ISNULL(VNAME)
? "NL_T12_the_cleared_bit_survived_close_and_reopen:" + (ALLTRIM(PLAIN) = "nulltwo")

; ============================================================================
; REFUSAL ONE -- a field with no null flag
; ============================================================================

GO 1
REPLACE PLAIN WITH NULL

GO 1
? "NL_T13_a_refused_null_left_the_non_nullable_value_intact:" + (ALLTRIM(PLAIN) = "control")

LOCATE FOR ISNULL(VNAME)
? "NL_T14_a_refused_null_left_the_rest_of_the_bitmap_intact:" + (ALLTRIM(PLAIN) = "nulltwo")

; ============================================================================
; REFUSAL TWO -- under table buffering. Runs last: it touches a session setting.
; ============================================================================

TABLE BUFFER ON

GO 1
REPLACE VNAME WITH NULL

TABLE BUFFER OFF

GO 1
? "NL_T15_a_buffered_null_was_refused_and_the_value_survived:" + (ALLTRIM(VNAME) = "alpha")

LOCATE FOR ISNULL(VNAME)
? "NL_T16_the_bitmap_survived_the_buffered_refusal:" + (ALLTRIM(PLAIN) = "nulltwo")

; ============================================================================
; UNGRADED PROBES -- what the '?' marker path actually does with ISNULL.
;
; NOT ARMS. Nothing above depends on them and they are placed last so that a
; FORMULA error here cannot cost a claim. They exist so the header's reasoning
; about eval_rhs is MEASURED on every run rather than inherited from a comment.
;
; The cursor is parked by the LOCATE above on rec 5, whose VNAME is null.
;
; PREDICTED, from reading the code and not from running it:
;   NL_P1 -- the bare form reaches eval_any and should print .T.
;   NL_P2 -- the concatenated form should NOT print ":.T.". Expected instead:
;            an empty tail, a number, or a FORMULA error. Whatever it prints is
;            the finding; record it rather than repairing it here.
;
; MEASURED 2026-09-05, FIRST RUN. NL_P1 printed `.T.`. NL_P2 printed `0` -- not
; the label, not a tail, a bare zero. That is Arith::evalString returning
; std::to_string(evalNumber(...)) over a string literal and a function that the
; scalar parser could not resolve. THE LABEL ITSELF WAS SWALLOWED: the marker did
; not merely lose its verdict, it lost its NAME, so a spec written this way would
; have printed one anonymous `0` per arm and a reader counting labels would have
; found nothing to count. The trace in the header is confirmed by measurement.
; ============================================================================

? "NL_P1_bare_isnull_marker_probe_the_next_line_is_the_answer"
? ISNULL(VNAME)
? "NL_P2_concatenated_isnull_probe:" + ISNULL(VNAME)

CLOSE
